er of your skin  so it doesn't get rubbed off as much and  it's not deep enough that it'll still be  near the surface it'll still be an  effective  screen evie uses a special patient that  involves a mix  of glycerin dimethicone and fatty acids  like lauric acid and stearic acid  this patient is actually used for other  things as well  so it's used in some treatments for  atopic dermatitis or eczema  and one really interesting thing about  this patient is that it can actually  increase your skin's resistance it  increases the barrier function  so it works as a better barrier and this  is very unusual for a penetration  enhancer because a lot of the time  penetration enhances that get things  deeper into your skin  will actually decrease your barrier  function so it opens it up a bit so that  things can get through  this is slightly different it seems to  deliver it and then seal it in  another bonus because this isn't a  barrier is that it also doesn't seem to  interfere with sweating  and it doesn't make you feel as hot the  biggest bonus with sunscreen of course  is that because  it's a little bit deeper it doesn't get  wiped away or washed away anywhere near  as easily  so there's a mechanism for why heavy  sunscreens might work better than other  sunscreens but  does it actually hold out in practice  evie is sold in sweden  and the eu and they tend to have higher  requirements for claims compared to  other countries they're also a pretty  famous brand in sweden they've won lots  of awards  so it's not really like they'd slip  under the radar of their regulators  so i asked evie if i could see their  tests and they were nice enough to show  me and they said i could share some of  the results here  i don't think they showed me all of  their tests because i think there's some  intellectual property stuff they want to  keep some stuff private  but yeah i'm really impressed by the  tests i've seen so far and i get the  feeling that they've done  a lot more tests on t